# Memory Index

<!--
  Anthropic load contract: the harness loads only the first 200 lines OR the
  first 25KB of this file (whichever comes first). Entries past either cap are
  silently dropped from the model's context at session start. The byte cap is
  the governing trigger (bites at ~110 entries). Keep each line ≤200 characters;
  cap total entries at ≤200; move detail into the topic file this index points
  to. The R-59 load guard (governance/mandatory-files-rules.json) counts both
  the raw line count AND the comment-stripped count and gates on the LARGER —
  these <!-- --> comment blocks do not buy unlimited headroom.
  Placement: pointer entries MUST sit above line 200 / 25KB or they load
  invisibly — the G1 placement guard (R-59 ordinal_position_check) warns.

  Two-axis taxonomy (see the memory-architecture docs spoke):
  - Filename prefix carries provenance: user_*, feedback_*, project_*,
    reference_*, episode_*. Use the prefix that matches origin.
  - `type:` frontmatter carries retrieval axis (semantic / procedural /
    episodic). The three sections below group by retrieval axis, most-loaded-
    first; `reflective` was dropped (reflection rides the feedback_ provenance
    prefix, not a separate retrieval type).

  When a fact spans axes, file under the dominant retrieval type (`type:`
  frontmatter wins) and link from other sections via [[wikilink]]
  cross-references — the memory tier is EXEMPT from the corpus link-grammar conversion
  (a [[name]] here resolves against the enumerated memory namespace, not the
  vault walk).

  Entry shape (≤200 chars/line):
  - [filename](memory/filename.md) — one-line retrieval hook starting with the
    rule, fact, or trigger (NOT a topic name).

  Vault-pointer shape (when an entry points into ~/.claude-plans/, the Obsidian
  vault, or a state path — NOT a memory/ sibling file): three elements —
  (1) the absolute path + (2) an imperative read-instruction (Read / Consult /
  Load / See) + (3) a decision-useful why. Worked exemplar:
    ~/.claude-plans/payments-migration/spec.md — Consult BEFORE any dependency analysis; this is the binding spec, not the working notes.
  Note the deliberate distinctness: a vault-pointer begins with a bare absolute
  path (no leading `- [`), whereas a relative-link entry begins with `- [`. That
  shape difference is load-bearing — the G1 placement guard keys on the bare-path
  imperative shape, so a relative-link `[filename](memory/...)` entry is never
  mistaken for a vault-pointer.

  Memory files MUST carry frontmatter conforming to schemas/memory-schema.json
  (required: name, description, type, tags, created, updated, last_validated).
  New writes auto-stamp updated + last_validated via hooks/pre-write-guard.sh +
  hooks/memory-auto-stamp.sh.
-->

